# WPNest — Tailwind CSS v4 Rules

## Overview

This project uses **Tailwind CSS v4** via the `@tailwindcss/vite` plugin.
The entry point is `sources/css/style.css`.  
**There is no SCSS** in this project.

## CSS File Structure

```text
sources/css/
├── style.css   → Entry: @import "tailwindcss", @source, @theme, @utility, WP alignment classes
└── menu.css    → Navigation: desktop nav, hamburger, mobile panel (imported by style.css)
```

Add new CSS files alongside these two only when a feature requires pseudo-element animations
or JS-driven state classes that **cannot** be expressed as Tailwind HTML utilities.
Always `@import` new files from `style.css`.

## Entry File (`style.css`) Sections — In Order

1. `@import "tailwindcss"` — Tailwind engine
2. `@source` — content scanning directives
3. `@theme {}` — design tokens
4. `@utility` — reusable utility definitions
5. Raw CSS — WordPress core class overrides (alignments, `.sticky`, etc.)
6. `@import` — additional CSS files (e.g. `menu.css`)

## Content Scanning

Always add `@source` for every file type that uses Tailwind classes:

```css
@source "../../**/*.php";
@source "../../**/*.js";
@source "../../sources/**/*.css";
```

## Design Tokens (`@theme`)

All colors, fonts, spacing tokens, and custom values live in `@theme {}`.

```css
@theme {
  --color-primary:   #1eb2a6;
  --color-secondary: #ffa34d;
  --color-gray-200:  #dee3e2;

  --font-sans: 'Open Sans', Arial, sans-serif;

  --height-banner:   300px;
  --shadow-header:   0 0 10px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
}
```

- **Never hardcode hex values** in PHP templates or CSS rules — always use `var(--color-*)`.
- Add new color tokens to `@theme` before using them.
- Token naming: `--color-*`, `--font-*`, `--height-*`, `--shadow-*`.

## `@utility` — Reusable Utilities

Use `@utility` for class strings that are too long to repeat, or semantically meaningful:

```css
@utility btn {
  @apply inline-block px-6 py-3 text-white font-bold bg-primary hover:bg-secondary
         rounded-sm transition-all duration-300 ease-in-out;
}

@utility container-wrap {
  @apply mx-auto px-5 lg:px-12 w-full;
  max-width: var(--width-container);
}
```

- Prefer `@apply` with existing Tailwind utilities inside `@utility`.
- Use `@utility` for: `btn`, `btn-sm`, `btn-link`, `pagination`, `skip-link`, `h-banner`, `max-w-container`, `shadow-header`, and block-specific wrappers.
- Do **not** create `@utility` for one-off layout — write Tailwind classes directly in HTML/PHP.

## Writing Tailwind Classes in PHP

- Write utility classes directly in `class=""` attributes.
- Use responsive prefixes inline: `class="text-base md:text-lg lg:text-xl"`.
- Use state variants inline: `class="text-black hover:text-primary transition-colors duration-300"`.
- For conditional classes, use PHP string concatenation or ternary — keep it readable.

## ACF Block Pattern

```html
<section <?php echo $wrapper_attributes; ?>>
    <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-5 lg:px-12 w-full">
        <div class="py-12 md:py-16">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-black mb-4">…</h2>
```

- Always use the `max-w-container` + `px-5 lg:px-12` container pattern.
- Use Tailwind responsive prefixes (`md:`, `lg:`) instead of media query mixins.
- Use `text-primary` / `text-secondary` — never hardcode colours.

## Raw CSS — When It's Acceptable

Write raw CSS **only** when Tailwind utilities cannot express the behaviour:

| Reason | Example |
|---|---|
| Pseudo-elements (`::before`, `::after`) | Hamburger bars, dropdown arrows |
| JS-driven state classes | `.is-open`, `.is-clicked` added by JS |
| WordPress core class names | `.alignleft`, `.sticky`, `.admin-bar` |
| Complex pointer-events toggling | Sub-menu visibility |

For all other cases, use Tailwind utility classes in markup.

## Responsive Design

Use Tailwind breakpoint prefixes. The `@theme` breakpoints map to:

| Prefix | Value |
|---|---|
| `sm:` | 576px |
| `md:` | 782px |
| `lg:` | 992px |
| `xl:` | 1200px |
| `xxl:` | 1400px |

## Never

- No SCSS / `.scss` files — the project has migrated to CSS + Tailwind v4.
- No `@use`, `@forward`, `rem()` mixins, or SCSS variables (`$var`).
- No hardcoded hex colors in templates or CSS rules.
- No inline `style=""` attributes for layout/spacing/color.
- No `!important` except for JS-toggled overrides (e.g. hamburger hide).
